# Search CTR week-over-week analysis

Windows are inclusive: 2026-09-21–2026-09-27 and 2026-09-28–2026-10-04.
Metric assumption (no metrics.json supplied): sum(clicks) / sum(searches), not mean of row CTRs.
The source does not document whether clicks means searches with a click or all click events.

Observed difference is after minus before. Adjusted difference uses platform × country strata and fixes each stratum's weight to the arithmetic mean of its two weekly search shares. Kitagawa decomposition: observed difference = adjusted within-stratum difference + mix difference. This is descriptive, not causal. Traffic mix itself might respond to the ranker.

Intervals are 95% normal binomial intervals, conditional on independent binary search outcomes; adjusted variance sums stratum variances multiplied by squared fixed weights. No user/session identifiers are available to estimate clustering. No uncertainty interval for a causal effect is identified.

All dates and expected platform × country cells are present. Count-based completeness checks pass when sufficient baseline exists; the first three dates cannot be tested due to insufficient history. There is no ETL watermark; load completeness is provisional. Search-volume completeness is also checked at both weekly endpoints. Integrity queries found no invalid counts or duplicate date/platform/country/ranker keys.

No joins were used: fanout skipped. No experimental assignment or planned allocation is available: SRM skipped. Ranker is perfectly confounded with week. The only release-log entry is the rollout; this is not proof that nothing else changed. No dashboard or independent source is present: reconciliation not done. No rollback threshold was supplied at analysis time.

Reproduce from the working directory: python3 analysis_output/analyze.py
SQL and retrieved aggregates: analysis_output/queries.json
Calculations, assumptions, and check outputs: analysis_output/results.json
Built-in checks (including query IDs): .ds-checks.log
